      "name": "asset_portability_context",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Adds a portability lens useful for policy/asset-management questions. Somewhat derivative of asset_category (laptop->mobile, desktop->fixed), but not a pure restatement. Values follow soundly from category readings."
    },
    {
      "name": "record_creation_signal",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A provenance dimension is potentially actionable. But distinctions like 'acquisition' vs 'registration' vs 'manual_provision' appear inferred from phrasing cues that the source text only weakly supports; 'automatic_generated' as the dominant value is not visibly grounded in the sampled rows, suggesting some fabrication."
    },
    {
      "name": "vendor_brand_primary",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Vendor breakdown is highly useful for spend consolidation. Dell/HP/Lenovo are well-grounded in text. However, mapping 'Cloud Database Service' to 'AWS' is an unwarranted confident specific where the text names no vendor, indicating some invented attribution."
    },
    {
      "name": "processing_lag_indicator",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.5,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A processing-delay flag could be actionable, but its meaning is opaque and it seems tied to whether processed_date exists (nan->False). As a bare boolean derived from date presence rather than an actual lag computation, its analytical leverage and grounding are limited."
    },
    {
      "name": "asset_specification_richness",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.4,
      "source_groundedness": 0.8,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Essentially a proxy for text specificity/category (high vs generic vs service_abstracted), largely redundant with asset_category and a near-metadata measure that adds little new analytical axis for expense actionability. Values track the source text reasonably."
    },
    {
      "name": "asset_expense_purpose",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.8,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Purpose classification (provisioning/travel/service/subscription/maintenance) directly supports understanding what spend is actionable. Somewhat correlated with category but adds a distinct intent dimension. Values are sound readings of the descriptions."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.72,
  "source_groundedness": 0.75,
  "rationale": "The set provides several genuinely useful analytical axes for expense analysis: category, vendor, and expense purpose are strong grouping/comparison dimensions well-grounded in the description and ci fields. Portability and specification-richness are more derivative of category, and processing_lag is a thin boolean, dragging usefulness down. Groundedness is generally good, but there are notable confident-specific hallucinations: inferring 'AWS' as vendor for a generic cloud service and the fine-grained record_creation_signal distinctions not clearly supported by the source text."
